{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_051e678d616ccb540169bb161cbc448191816770d8d701b4f1", "created": 1773868572, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**METASTASIS** is the process/event in which cancer spreads from its primary (original) tumor site to other parts of the body. During metastasis, cancer cells break away from the primary tumor, travel through the bloodstream or the lymphatic system (and associated vessels), and establish new growths (metastases) in other organs or tissues, effectively forming additional tumors. Metastasis can involve spread to regional lymph nodes and/or distant organs such as the lungs, liver, bones, or distant lymph nodes; examples noted include prostate cancer spreading beyond the prostate to lymph nodes or bones, bladder cancer spreading outside the bladder, anal cancer spreading to the liver or lungs, colon cancer spreading to organs outside the colon, and cervical cancer spreading from the pelvis to distant sites.\n\nMetastasis is central to cancer classification and staging. It defines “metastatic” or very advanced disease and is commonly associated with stage 4 (Stage IV) cancer; for example, distant spread corresponds to stage IV anal cancer and stage 4B cervical cancer. In the TNM staging system, metastasis is captured by the **“M”** category, typically summarized as **M0** (no distant metastasis) versus **M1** (distant metastasis present), with some systems further subcategorizing sites (e.g., lung as M1a and bone/other sites as M1b). While regional lymph node involvement is often reflected in the **“N”** component, the **“M”** component specifically indicates distant spread.\n\nDetermining whether cancer has metastasized—and, in some contexts, whether metastatic risk is low or high—is commonly done using imaging, including PET (such as PSMA/PET in prostate cancer), MRI, bone scans, and other modalities. When imaging suggests spread, metastatic sites may serve as sources of tissue for testing, and biopsy of one or multiple suspected metastatic lesions may be required to confirm cancer at the new site. Metastasis may be present at diagnosis (initial advanced disease) or occur after treatment, in which case it may be described as distant recurrence. Although metastasis is uncommon in some cancers (e.g., squamous cell skin cancer), its presence is clinically important because it strongly influences treatment decisions and prognosis.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 462, "prompt_tokens": 1038, "total_tokens": 1500, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}